# Prism development rules

These rules permanently govern all work in this repository.

## Source of truth

- Read the build specification (`CURSOR-BUILD-SPEC-AI-MUSIC-VISUALIZER.md`) before planning or implementing features.
- Treat the build specification as authoritative for product scope, architecture, constraints, and phase acceptance criteria.
- If instructions conflict, prefer the build specification and these rules over assumptions.

## Phase discipline

- Work on only the explicitly requested phase (1A–1G or a later named phase).
- Never continue into another phase without user approval.
- After every phase: run formatting, lint, type-checking, tests, and a production build; summarize results; then stop and wait.
- Do not “helpfully” scaffold future-phase packages, providers, or screens unless the current phase requires them.

## Code change hygiene

- Inspect existing code before editing.
- Preserve unrelated work; do not rewrite clean code for style-only preferences.
- Ask before making major architecture changes (new apps, new top-level packages, protocol redesigns, provider choices, schema breaks).
- Prefer small, reviewable diffs aligned to the active phase acceptance criteria.

## TypeScript and validation

- Use strict TypeScript and avoid `any`. If an escape hatch seems unavoidable, stop and ask first.
- Validate network and realtime boundaries with Zod schemas from `packages/contracts` (or the phase-appropriate contracts location).
- Keep shared domain types and protocol messages in contracts—not duplicated ad hoc in UI files.

## Security and privacy

- Never expose secrets to clients. No service-role keys, AI keys, or other secrets in `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` or client bundles.
- Never invent unsupported music-provider capabilities. No production Spotify / Apple Music / SoundCloud / Pandora integrations unless a later approved phase explicitly adds them.
- Never record, save, or transmit microphone audio. Live Listen is on-device analysis only; only numeric feature frames may cross process/network boundaries.
- Prism must not stream, download, proxy, record, or redistribute copyrighted music.

## Architecture boundaries

- Keep music providers behind `packages/provider-adapters`.
- Keep AI providers behind `packages/ai-adapters`.
- Visualizers must implement the plugin contract and dispose all GPU/resources.
- Audio-engine, visual-engine, and sync-engine stay package-bounded; apps orchestrate, packages implement.

## Resource lifecycle and UX states

- Dispose audio nodes, media elements, WebGL/Three resources, subscriptions, timers, and event listeners on unmount/route change.
- Include loading, empty, denied-permission, offline, error, and fallback states for every touched flow that can fail or wait.

## Completion checklist (every phase)

Before claiming completion:

1. Run formatting, lint, type-checking, tests, and a production build.
2. Confirm the phase acceptance criteria in the build specification.
3. Report:
   - files changed
   - commands run
   - results
   - manual verification steps
   - limitations
   - recommended next phase
4. Stop and wait for approval before continuing.

## Explicit non-goals unless approved

- Do not initialize or expand product scope beyond the requested phase.
- Do not add billing, social feeds, lyrics, creator marketplace, full AI video generation, or native desktop system-audio capture.
- Do not add Tizen, webOS, Roku, or Apple TV apps during MVP phases.
